WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.088 --> 00:00:08.629
In the previous unit, we talked
about the assembly process and, and

2
00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:12.712
logic in general, and
about writing assemblers in general.

3
00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:17.705
And in this unit, we will start
the journey of actually building

4
00:00:17.705 --> 00:00:21.033
an assembler, an assembler that works and

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:27.720
that can translate programs from symbolic
hack code into binary Hack code.

6
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.250
So here is the overall
picture in a nutshell.

7
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:37.270
We are given a source program written
in symbolic Hack language and

8
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:43.020
we have to translate it into an equivalent
program written in binary code.

9
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:48.570
So if you were the person who's supposed
to write to develop such an assembler,

10
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.530
how would you go about it?

11
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:54.030
And what, what is the knowledge
that you have to know in order to

12
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.790
develop such a general purpose translator?

13
00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.642
Because bear in mind, we have to be able
to translate any program written in Hack

14
00:01:01.642 --> 00:01:06.130
symbolic code, not just the subset
of programs but any given program.

15
00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.630
Well, this is, in general,
it's a translation challenge, right?

16
00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:14.110
You have to translate from one language,
from the source language,

17
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.330
to the target language.

18
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.880
And in order to do this, you have to
know the grammar of both languages.

19
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.740
You have to know the syntax rules.

20
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.650
For example, if you want to translate
from French to Spanish, you have to know

21
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.780
the grammatical rules of French in
order to take the source sentence and

22
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.650
decompose it or
parse it into its lingual elements.

23
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.960
Then you observe or
you inspect these elements.

24
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.640
You figure out what is
the semantics of this sentence, and

25
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.410
then you re-express
the semantics in Spanish.

26
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.680
And in order to do this, you have to
know the rules of the game in Spanish.

27
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:55.930
You have to be able to synthesize a target
sentence written in the target language.

28
00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.530
And that's exactly what we do here.

29
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.219
However, when we write translators for
programming languages,

30
00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:08.456
the task is supremely easier because
programming languages are supremely

31
00:02:08.456 --> 00:02:13.086
simpler than human spoken languages or
natural languages.

32
00:02:13.086 --> 00:02:19.334
And in particular, machine languages
are are very simple, and therefore

33
00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:25.586
the translation task is going to be less
formidable as it may seem at the outset.

34
00:02:25.586 --> 00:02:31.503
All right, so what do we have
to know about the Hack language?

35
00:02:31.503 --> 00:02:35.404
Well, the Hack language
consists of A-instructions,

36
00:02:35.404 --> 00:02:37.586
C-instructions, and symbols.

37
00:02:37.586 --> 00:02:42.469
So let us explore every one of
these categories very briefly.

38
00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:47.710
The A-instruction comes up
in in its symbolic syntax.

39
00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.750
And here is the equivalent binary syntax.

40
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.890
And if you want to
translate one to the other,

41
00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:59.140
I think it doesn't take too much
imagination to figure out how to do it.

42
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.320
And in any rate, we are going to
say it explicitly down the road.

43
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.684
The C-instruction in a very similar
fashion, has a symbolic syntax and

44
00:03:07.684 --> 00:03:09.210
a binary syntax.

45
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:14.870
And there's a set of tables that describe
the mapping between the former and

46
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.160
the, the latter.

47
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.115
And finally the Hack
language features symbols.

48
00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:24.360
You know, the programmer is invited
to use some pre-defined symbols.

49
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.740
And also, the programmer is invited
to invent symbols of his or

50
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:34.886
her own using the label
declaration statement and

51
00:03:34.886 --> 00:03:42.250
eth statements or A-instructions that
are designed to declare and use variables.

52
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:47.230
So this, basically, you know,
this is a complete description

53
00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:51.520
of the Hack machine language and
given this description,

54
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.050
you should be able to develop
the desired assembler.

55
00:03:56.050 --> 00:04:01.250
So given that we have a program
in which all these things

56
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:05.350
that I mentioned before come to play in a,
in a vivid example.

57
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.650
What exactly do we have here, you know,
in terms of general constructs?

58
00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.060
Well, first of all, we have white space.

59
00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.330
We haven't mentioned white space in the
language specification, we should have.

60
00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.920
But the white space is almost the same in
every language that you can think about.

61
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.980
You are welcome to introduce
empty lines and comments and

62
00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:35.870
in the Hack language, a comment is
anything that begins with two slashes.

63
00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:38.680
So we have to know how to
deal with white space.

64
00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.080
And then we have a sequence of
A-instructions and C-instructions

65
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.400
that we have to translate, and finally,
we have to deal with the symbols.

66
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.010
Symbols come to play either in
label declaration commands or

67
00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.300
in references in the context
of A-instructions.

68
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.850
So, to make a long story short, if you
want to write an assembler, you have to

69
00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:04.689
know how to deal with white space,
with instructions, and with symbols.

70
00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:11.730
Let me say a few words about symbols
before we before we go along.

71
00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:19.010
I've used red ink in what you see here in
order to highlight all the symbols that

72
00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.860
appear in this program and it's, as you
can see, there are quite a few of them.

73
00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:27.570
And that's quite characteristic to
symbolic machine language programs.

74
00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.070
They have numerous symbols in them.

75
00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.440
So the question is,
how do we deal with these symbols?

76
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.700
Well you know, the answer is that
in many cases in life when you face

77
00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:46.450
a big mountain in front of your
track well, in some cases,

78
00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:51.010
you know, the best way to deal with this
mountain Is simply to, to bypass it.

79
00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.450
And that's exactly what I'm
going to do with the symbols.

80
00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:55.440
I'm going to deal with them later.

81
00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.860
And by the way, if we are lucky,
as we bypass this mountain,

82
00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.860
we may gain some ground and
then summiting the mountain

83
00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.640
may be easier from the other side
than from the original side.

84
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.950
And indeed, you will see that when we'll
introduce symbols later on in the process,

85
00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:18.390
it will be much easier to to morph them,
so to speak, into our assembler solution.

86
00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.850
All right, so symbols later and
with that in mind,

87
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:27.290
I now have to deal only with
programs that have no symbols.

88
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.690
And the assembler that I have
to write is much simpler.

89
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:35.230
It's an assembler that deals with
symbol-less symbolic Hack programs.

90
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:41.370
So the challenge that remain is to deal
with white space and instructions only.

91
00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.135
And let us begin with white space.

92
00:06:44.135 --> 00:06:47.840
Now, what should we do
with with white space?

93
00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.650
Once again, I've used red ink to highlight
all the white space in the program.

94
00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:57.080
And the easiest way to
deal with white space and

95
00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.780
obviously the most sensible
way is to simply ignore it.

96
00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:07.420
So as you process the source code
whenever you see a comment or

97
00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:11.300
an empty line,
you simply toss away all this

98
00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:16.520
all the contents the follow,
that follow in this specific line.

99
00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.420
And you go on to translate
the remaining code.

100
00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:25.452
So white space is simply ignored.

101
00:07:25.452 --> 00:07:28.782
So what remains after we eliminate

102
00:07:28.782 --> 00:07:32.213
eliminate the white space
is only instructions.

103
00:07:32.213 --> 00:07:39.714
So the challenge is reduced to translating
programs that have pure instructions,

104
00:07:39.714 --> 00:07:44.012
so to speak, and
these are this is a sequence or

105
00:07:44.012 --> 00:07:48.045
a stream of A-instructions and
C-instructions.

106
00:07:48.045 --> 00:07:49.873
And in order to translate them,

107
00:07:49.873 --> 00:07:54.900
we have to know how to translate
these instructions into binary code.

108
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.480
And that's exactly what we
are going to do in the next unit.

109
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.230
But before we introduce the next unit,

110
00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:09.660
I'd like to give you an overall picture
of where we're heading from here onward.

111
00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.870
Well here's the, here's the plan.

112
00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:18.750
Since we decided to defer the treatment
of symbols to a later stage,

113
00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:22.450
we'll begin by writing a basic assembler

114
00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:27.010
that can deal with programs
that contain no symbols.

115
00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:29.000
This will be done in the next unit.

116
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:33.920
In the subsequent unit, we are going
to develop the ability to represent,

117
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.780
handle, and use symbols.

118
00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:41.380
And finally, using this ability, we
are going to return to the basic assembler

119
00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.180
that we wrote before and morph it or

120
00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:49.915
extend it into a general purpose assembler
that can translate any given program,

121
00:08:49.915 --> 00:08:55.380
any given program written in Hack
assembly code into binary code.

122
00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.960
This is the plan.

123
00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.250
So beginning with the next unit,

124
00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.680
we're going to talk about how
to translate instructions.